", (2, 1), S+W); label(\"$x$\", (2, -1), N+N+N+W);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (5.5+.3, .5-.3), S); label(\"$y$\", (6.5+.3, 0)); label(\"$y$\", (5+.5, -1.5+.3));\nlabel(\"$110^{\\circ}$\",(2.5,3.5)); label(\"$100^{\\circ}$\",(6.5-.2,3.5));\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f00cfcb8cfd2c96605522b067b790f8f5ac29f62a2f95bc1c559688fa1720255"},"seconds":34.57881760597229,"seed":1059719570606870731,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the degree measure of angle \\(AFD\\), we need to use the information that angles \\(BAD\\) and \\(CDA\\) are trisected. Let's denote the trisected angle \\(BAD\\) as \\(3x\\) and the trisected angle \\(CDA\\) as \\(3y\\). According to the diagram, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAD = 3x = 110^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle CDA = 3y = 100^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can find the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{110^\\circ}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = \\frac{100^\\circ}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the measure of angle \\(AFD\\). Notice that \\(AFD\\) is an exterior angle to the triangle \\(AFD\\) formed by the lines \\(AF\\) and \\(DF\\). The measure of angle \\(AFD\\) can be found by considering the sum of the interior angles of quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\).\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDA = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(\\angle BAD = 110^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle CDA = 100^\\circ\\). Let \\(\\angle ABC = a\\) and \\(\\angle BCD = b\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n110^\\circ + a + b + 100^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\na + b = 150^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, consider the angles around point \\(F\\). The angle \\(AFD\\) is the sum of the two angles \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) because \\(AF\\) and \\(DF\\) trisect the angles \\(BAD\\) and \\(CDA\\) respectively. Therefore, the measure of angle \\(AFD\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AFD = x + y = \\frac{110^\\circ}{3} + \\frac{100^\\circ}{3} = \\frac{210^\\circ}{3} = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the degree measure of angle \\(AFD\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{70}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{70}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
